  :adr/body "## What was wrong

`itonami` with no arguments printed eight lines of help and exited 0. Every
other agent CLI on this machine -- `claude`, `hermes-agent` -- opens its REPL
there, and names on that first screen what it can actually do.

The help text had already begun promising the REPL. The line
`itonami                        chat REPL を開く` shipped in the resident
release while `-main` still matched `(empty? named)` to the help branch and
exited. The screen and the code disagreed, and the screen was the one that
lied.

Measured 2026-09-06: `itonami` in a terminal printed help; `itonami chat`
printed a single banner line and a prompt. The REPL existed. Nothing pointed
at it, and the one thing that claimed to did not.

## Decision

1. Argless `itonami` on a TTY opens the REPL, by way of the same `chat!`
   entry `itonami chat` uses. There is no second path into the loop.
2. Argless under a pipe, a non-TTY, or with `--json` still prints help and
   exits 0. A REPL with nobody at it would hang a script that used to get
   help, and the help now says this in its own text rather than leaving a
   caller to discover it.
3. `help` and `--help` keep printing help on a TTY.
4. The REPL opens on a start screen: a wordmark, a framed panel naming the
   command inventory (largest first-word groups of the 209 generated commands,
   the named aliases, the slash commands), the resolved profile and where it
   came from, the working directory, where the session token was found, and
   the counts.
5. The screen is a harness plugin (`:ctx/splash`, ADR-2609042200), so
   `--dump-config` and `/plugins` name it like every other layer.

## What the screen is allowed to say

Every line is read from something this process already looked up, plus one
measured `/health`. `/health` is awaited rather than printed late, because a
warning that arrives after the prompt lands in the middle of the operator's
first line.

The server line has three states, not two: answering (with the store id it
reported), refused, and -- when the request could not be made at all -- said
as such. CLAUDE.md's sixth question is the reason: a check that could not run
must not return the value of a check that ran and passed. The session line
names *which* source answered (environment variable or Keychain item), because
a stale export and a fresh login produce an identical working CLI right up to
the moment the exported one expires.

The tail of every truncated list is named (`+20 more: business · projects ·
…`), not dropped. A screen that lists five of twenty-five groups and says
nothing about the other twenty is telling the operator those twenty do not
exist.

## Width

`display-width` counts East Asian Wide and Fullwidth as two columns and
everything else -- box drawing and block elements included, which UAX#11 calls
Ambiguous -- as one, which is what a Latin-locale terminal does. Warnings print
below the frame, where a glyph whose width a terminal disagrees about cannot
push a border off.

Two checks, and neither covers the other:

- `width-counts-columns-not-code-units` pins the ruler against literal column
  counts. Control (measured): making `wide?` answer false for everything fails
  it.
- `every-frame-row-is-the-same-width` pins the frame arithmetic against the
  ruler. Control (measured): an off-by-one in the title fill fails it -- and
  that off-by-one was really there when the test was first run.

The frame check does NOT catch a wrong ruler: it pads and asserts with the
same function, so the ruler grades itself. That is written down in the test
file, next to the assertion, so a later reader does not mistake it for
coverage it does not have.

## The theme injection is load-bearing

`itonami-profile` records what a declared-but-unread `:inject` cost the last
time: `:skin` in configuration selected nothing while `/skin` worked, and every
assertion in reach only checked that mounting had succeeded. So the splash
layer reads its injected theme at render time -- the skin's banner is the
tagline under the wordmark, the skin's accent glyph is the separator the counts
line is joined with -- and the test asserts it through the rendered screen in
both directions: the kawaii banner present, the default banner gone.

## A fix that came with it

Ctrl+D rejects the pending `readline.question`. Nothing caught that rejection,
so leaving the REPL the ordinary way printed an AbortError and an nbb stack
trace over the operator's last screen -- a crash report for a keystroke that
means \"I am done\". It was survivable while the REPL was something you had to
ask for by name; making it the argless entry makes Ctrl+D the common exit. It
now exits 0 in silence.

## Rejected: merging the hermes-parity branch instead

`~/.cloud-itonami/app` sits on `agent/harness-plugin-architecture`, 11 commits
ahead of `main` and 41 behind, and the running release
(`releases/dfddb86b…-clean`) was built from it. That branch carries a second
implementation of the same plugin harness `main` landed at 93d9972, plus a
larger set of hermes-parity slash commands, in files (`itonami_chat.cljs`,
`itonami_theme.cljs`) that `main` does not have. Merging it would land two
harnesses and decide, in passing, a question nobody asked here.

So this change is written against `origin/main` and takes nothing from that
branch. Its slash commands remain worth landing on their own terms; that is a
separate decision, and it is still open. Note that the running release is
therefore NOT what `main` builds: an operator reading `itonami --help` from the
resident install is reading the branch's text, not this one, until the release
is rebuilt.

## Consequences

- A script calling bare `itonami` and reading stdout keeps working: the pipe
  case is unchanged.
- A person calling bare `itonami` gets the REPL, and pays one `/health`
  request (a few milliseconds against a live resident, and a refused connect
  when there is none) before the prompt.
- `bin/itonami_splash.cljk` is a new sibling of the launcher. The single-file
  resident copy is unaffected: `--print-data-dir` still answers before any
  sibling is required, which is the property `launcher_test` measures, and it
  was re-measured by hand here.
